{"name":"Royal Navy Blue","hex":"#0066cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"navy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A country's entire maritime military force, including ships and personnel.","A governmental department in charge of a country's maritime military force."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the dark blue colour of navy blue.","Belonging to the navy; typical of the navy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/navy"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navy blue","blocks":[{"text":"Navy blue, also known simply as navy, is a dark shade of the color blue. The name navy blue originally referred to the color of uniforms worn by officers in the Royal Navy. In the late 18th century, the Royal Navy adopted the color for its sailors' uniforms, partly due to the practical reason that dark colors were less prone to showing dirt and wear during long sea voyages. The color became so associated with naval service that it came to be known simply as \"navy blue.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg/330px-Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy_blue","wikidata":"Q5975887"},{"title":"Royal Navy","blocks":[{"text":"The Royal Navy (RN) is the naval warfare force of the United Kingdom, responsible for defending the country, the Crown Dependencies, and the Overseas Territories from naval attack or invasion. It is a component of His Majesty's Naval Service, and its officers hold their commissions from the King. Although warships were used by English and Scottish kings from the early medieval period, the first major maritime engagements were fought in the Hundred Years' War against France. The modern Royal Navy traces its origins to the English Navy of the early 16th century, making it the oldest of the United Kingdom's armed services. It is consequently known as the Senior Service."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/89/Logo_of_the_Royal_Navy.svg/330px-Logo_of_the_Royal_Navy.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Navy","wikidata":"Q172771"},{"title":"Navy","blocks":[{"text":"A navy, naval force, military maritime fleet, war navy, or maritime force is the branch of a state's armed forces principally designated for naval and amphibious warfare; namely, lake-borne, riverine, littoral, or ocean-borne combat operations and related functions. It includes anything conducted by surface ships, amphibious ships, submarines, and seaborne aviation, as well as ancillary support, communications, training, and other fields."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg/330px-The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy","wikidata":"Q4508"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Blue_with_deep_sky.webp/500px-Blue_with_deep_sky.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Blue_with_deep_sky.webp/500px-Blue_with_deep_sky.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_with_deep_sky.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg/500px-Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg/500px-Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWertheimer_Optimist_(12).jpg"},{"srcset":"https